# Line endings are the repository's, not each clone's config.
#
# Nothing here is being repaired. Every blob and every working copy is already LF —
# `git cat-file -p HEAD:src/lib.rs | tr -cd '\r' | wc -c` is nought, and `git add --renormalize .`
# changed no file. What this does is stop that depending on whoever cloned: `core.autocrlf` is a
# per-machine setting, and a clone without it would commit CRLF into a repository that has none.
#
# `text=auto` stores LF and hands out the platform's own on checkout, so a Windows editor still sees
# CRLF and the object database stops caring who typed.
#
# The "LF will be replaced by CRLF" line git prints on commit is `core.autocrlf` announcing what it
# will do at the *next checkout*. It is not a warning about the content, and this file does not
# silence it.
* text=auto

# Rust, TOML and Markdown are text whatever the heuristic thinks.
*.rs   text
*.toml text
*.md   text
